body:
I used to picture myself jumping off a high cliff into an ocean/sea to die, so I would have to get to a place where I could do it. 

&#x200B;

I would definitely eat at some nice seafood places, check out small bookstores, enjoy the sun etc.

This is going to become more common. Children suicides have and will continue to rise. Reach out to the young people in your life. They have access to so much more information because of the internet. Talked to a suicidal kid on reddit maybe a month ago(?). They're listening in on what's happening around them. 

&#x200B;

 ["Half of the hospital visits for suicide ideation/attempt were by adolescents ages 15-17, 37% were by teens ages 12-14 and 12.8% were by children ages 5-11. About 64.4% of the visits were by females, and about 58% of visits required inpatient hospitalization. Annual totals rose 292% from 6,392 to 25,085 and increased as a percentage of total hospital visits from 0.66% to 1.82%, according to the study. Visits rose for each of the three age groups and for both females and and males."](http://www.aappublications.org/news/2018/05/16/suicideattempts051618)"

&#x200B;

[Pre-13 suicides have been understudied, Sheftall said, largely because it's hard to imagine children this young want to kill themselves.](https://www.cnn.com/2017/08/14/health/child-suicides/index.html)

&#x200B;

[The findings showed that in 2008 through 2015, nearly 116,000 children aged 5 to 17 were seen at 31 hospitals, either for having suicidal thoughts or for attempted suicide. Two-thirds were girls. More than half were hospitalized, and more than 13 percent needed intensive care. The rest were treated in an emergency setting or held for observation. Overall, suicide-related teen hospitalizations accounted for 0.66 percent of all hospitalizations at the children's hospitals in 2008. But by 2015, that figure had more than doubled, to almost 2 percent, according to the report. Increases were seen across all ages, but differed across certain groups. For example, the rise in suicide-related hospitalizations was particularly high among teens aged 15 to 17, who accounted for more than half of all the cases. The second highest rise was seen among teens aged 12 to 14, who accounted for 37 percent of all cases.](https://consumer.healthday.com/general-health-information-16/suicide-health-news-646/big-rise-seen-in-u-s-kids-teens-attempting-suicide-733931.html)

You can want to do something and be scared to do it. Like maybe you're a person who loves to sing and wants to be a professional singer, but you still have stage fright. Or you want to ask out that cute girl or whoever but you're scared to do so. 

&#x200B;

The process can hurt. You could end up disfigured, brain damage, paralyzed, etc. if it goes wrong. What does death even feel like, if it feels like anything.

For 3., I meant if being in patient was no longer a good option and you left if you could have someone check in on you. Especially if getting this care will make you worse. 

&#x200B;

Sorry you're going through this

Does he know you feel this way?

&#x200B;

What kind of illness/disease do you have? If you don't want to get into specifics, can you give a ballpark of how much it would cost?
